{
  "requirement": "The book preprocessor MUST escape requirement names and link targets when inserting badge HTML. It MUST encode badge label and message text in Shields.io URL path components so punctuation cannot change the badge URL structure.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.references.safe_badges`. I found no gap.\n\n**Obligations derived from the requirement**\n1. Requirement names and link targets MUST be HTML-escaped when badge HTML is inserted.\n2. Badge label and message text MUST be encoded in Shields.io URL path components, so punctuation cannot change the URL structure.\n\n**Implementation consulted**\nI read the two items marked `{impl}` for this requirement, to see which paths the tests need to reach:\n- `src/lib.rs#fn[replace_definitions]` escapes the name and the detail-page link target (`escape_html(&chapter_href(...))`).\n- `src/lib.rs#fn[reference_badge]` escapes the reference href and the built-in's title text.\n- `src/lib.rs#fn[shield_badge]` percent-encodes the label and message in the URL path and HTML-escapes the `src` URL and the `alt` text.\n\n**Test mapping**\n\n*Obligation 2, Shields URL path encoding*\n- `badge_urls_percent_encode_punctuation_and_html_is_escaped` calls `shield_badge(\"a/b?c\", \"x#y%z&w<v>\\\"u' café\", ...)`. It asserts the exact URL. `/`, `?`, `#`, `%`, `&`, `<`, `>`, `\"`, `'` and non-ASCII are percent-encoded. Spaces become `_`. The `&` in the query is escaped to `&amp;` in the attribute.\n- The same test asserts the HTML-escaped `alt` text.\n- `shields_badge_urls_escape_requirement_names` checks the Shields-specific escapes: `_` becomes `__` and `-` becomes `--`. It also checks that a space in the message becomes `_` (`Determination_false`).\n\n*Obligation 1, HTML escaping of link targets*\n- `definition_evidence_link_targets_are_escaped` renders a requirement-index chapter whose path is `a&b \"q\" <x>.md`. It asserts the escaped `href` (`a&amp;b &quot;q&quot; &lt;x&gt;/rules.ready.html`). It also asserts that the raw `a&b`, `\"q\"` and `<x>` do not appear in the name link. This covers the `replace_definitions` href path.\n- `reference_link_targets_are_escaped` renders a `{cite}` badge pointing at a target chapter whose rendered path contains `&`, `\"`, `<` and `>`. It asserts the escaped `href` and that the raw characters are absent. This covers the `reference_badge` href path.\n\n*Obligation 1, requirement names*\n- Valid names cannot contain HTML-special characters. The implementation comment says the name escaping is only a guard against future name rules, so it cannot be tested through the public path.\n- Name text in the badge is checked through the `alt` escaping in `shield_badge`, and through the rendered name and title assertions in other tests.\n\n**Unmarked related tests**\n- The built-in badge title escaping in `reference_badge` is checked in `fenced_references_render_their_badge_and_body_and_built_ins_are_unlinked`. That test compares against `escape_html(...)` itself, so it is weak. The escaping function is independently verified by the tests above.\n- The term link in `replace_inline_references` is not a badge and is not marked `{impl}` for this requirement.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "f360114568ba3231c5da9e9b2c91d05137e8af13c41c17d23dea91167c755e21",
